breaking news from ukraine ukrainian  president zielinski says at least 15  people have died in a russian airstrike  on a train station in the central part  of the country this attack  on a day laden with both hope and sorrow  today is independence day the national  holiday marking independence from the  former soviet union today also marks six  months of the russian invasion now in a  moment we're going to have a report on a  nation that was scheduled to celebrate  freedom before a war got in the way  first though we want to go to dw's  matthias berlinger he is in  kiev so we've got this report matthias  on this attack on a train station in the  central part of the country what more  can you tell us  yeah we've just heard that uh 15 people  were killed and several persons wounded  in that attack now today is a day where  many people have expected such attacks  to happen  possibly even on the capital of kiev  this has not happened so far but we've  also heard that quite a few rockets have  been intercepted by air defense by  ukrainian air defense and uh it's not  impossible that some were meant for here  for the for the government buildings  that russia has threatened to attack or  that at least some russian  propagandists have threatened to attack  and we've also seen rockets come down in  other parts of the country in the more  western uhnitsuki region and uh also in  kharkiv we've heard  about explosions so it's clear that  russia is sending a signal at this day  and uh it is uh that and that people  have suffered from russian attacks again  in different parts of the country okay  matias stand by this attack comes as we  said earlier on this independence day in  ukraine celebrations were banned in the  capital kiev for fears of a russian  attack we have this report now on how  ukrainians are finding ways to show  their resistance  russian tanks are lined up in the  ukrainian capital  the message  this is the only way russian military  equipment will appear in kiev  burnt and destroyed  it was unusually quiet as ukrainians  mark their independence day in the  capital  occasionally air raid warnings disrupted  the silence  [Music]  but the mood in the city is defiant  i'm very proud of our country  i'm glad that we came back from europe  where we had fled to to celebrate here  now on this day with our fellow citizens  i believe victory will come  it's a special day for me today  i feel nothing  no fear  i traveled almost 100 kilometers here  from near keith  nothing scares me  you just have  what for us is the end of the war  we used to say peace now we say victory  we won't look for a mutual understanding  with a terrorist even though we  understand the russian language you came  to defend while killing thousands of  people you came to liberate  we finally became united  a new nation emerged on february 24th at  4am  not born  but reborn  a nation that did not cry scream or get  scared one that did not run away did not  give up and did not forget  the president and first lady also  visited a memorial to the soldiers  killed in the conflict  meanwhile ukrainian troops in the east  are locked in a grinding war of  attrition with the russian army  almighty of sperlinger still with us  matias on this independent today we're  talking about a possible war of  attrition now six months into this  conflict  is there anything for ukrainians to  celebrate  well this war of attrition has been  going on for several months now and uh  it's the the fear is that it's  going to be a war of exhaustion actually  and of course then many worries occur  how long will the west sustain its  support to ukraine etc ukraine is making  of course uh sometimes even spectacular  strikes against russian ammunition  depots or supply infrastructures  we've seen these strikes on crimea we  have seen several strikes in the hassan  region mainly in the south where ukraine  was able to take out  seemingly big uh ammunition dumps  which very spectacular explosions etc um  so ukraine is in a better position now  than it was three weeks ago or three  months ago uh when it was just pounded  by this these uh this russian firepower  that was 10 times people estimated at  the time higher than the ukrainian but  still we are not seeing any territorial  or significant territorial gains by the  ukrainians they have been able already a  few months ago to drive back the  russians from some of the positions that  were very close to the cities of kharkiv  and nick mcalive and also of course  the retreat from the kiev area but since  then things are really grindingly slow  the russian advance is also slow but it  is  consistent so  we it doesn't look like  there is going to be a fast turn anytime  soon in this war in martial we have  heard before and during this war  particularly from ultranationalists in  russia that that ukraine is not even a  real  country what has the russian invasion  what has it done  to strengthen ukrainian identity  well that's not only ultra nationalist  that's also putin who has said that um  he has clearly miscalculated of course  the ukrainians saw themselves as a  nation before  what it has done is that it has further  consolidated the feeling of the nation  and maybe also the feeling of a nation  that is united i mean you can't be a  nation and you can be quarreling all  over with everybody the elder with the  younger and the easterners with the  westerners the russian speakers with the  ukrainian speakers and i think after  this 24th of february what has been  deluded is this maybe mistrust of groups  against each other there was a process  that was already on the way at least  since 2014 but also maybe since the  1990s it's been accelerating over the  time but the  24th of february has really  boosted this feeling of of of unity here  and people perceive themselves as more  united than before  dw's matias berlinger with the latest  tonight on this independence day  in kiev  well so far ukraine has been spared that  feared massive invasion by the russian  military dw's william bluecroft takes a  look back at how the war started  and at some of its major turning points  after months of speculation warnings  fears and a massive troop buildup on  three sides of ukraine russian president  vladimir putin finally revealed his  deadly intentions on february 24th first  let's look at how things were on the eve  of the war now russia already occupied  the korean peninsula in the south in  2014 and at the same time helped  separatists take control of part of  ukraine's eastern donbus region now in  that conflict the united nations  estimates 14 000 civilians have been  killed in the years since  now starting february 24th putin  launched a full full-scale invasion of  ukraine by land by sea and by air the  russian leader claims the ukrainian  government has committed crimes against  russian-speaking ukrainians but outside  russia the war is seen as a desperate  effort to stop ukraine's further  integration with the west which putin  views as a threat to his power  now in the first stage of the war until  around the beginning of april russian  forces pounded the capital kiev and  other major urban centers like hakeev  and maria paul entire civilian areas  were flattened millions of people fled  to neighboring countries or to ukraine's  somewhat safer western region  but russia underestimated ukraine's  resistance and its military couldn't  establish air superiority it tried but  failed to surround kiev and decapitate  ukraine's democratically elected  government so after several weeks of  little progress from the north of the  country russian commanders ordered a  withdrawal to concentrate their  firepower on the east  that is the war's second phase ukrainian  troops were outmanned and outgunned and  they were forced to steadily retreat  allowing russian forces to consolidate  gains on the ground now what you see  here doesn't mean that russia has full  control of these areas what we can say  is that in the south and the east is  where russia has advanced the most and  exerts some level of administrative  control it's these areas that have seen  the heaviest fighting  and now we're in a third phase ukraine  says it's fighting back with the help of  us and other western weapons  both sides have suffered tremendous  losses though it's impossible to put a  precise figure on that same goes for the  civilian toll which the united nations  officially puts at thirteen thousand  killed and wounded but they acknowledge  it's likely a gross undercut now it's  unclear what putin's end game is here as  the war becomes one of attrition whether  ukraine has the strength to push back  against russian advances whether russia  has the resources to establish itself as  an occupying power and whether the  winter ahead will freeze fighting and  force some kind of deal  that was dw's william gluecroft there  i'm joined now by maria abdiva she is a  security analyst based in harkief in the  northeast of ukraine when russian troops  attacked her hometown early in this war  she started collecting evidence of  possible war crimes and she committed  herself to fighting russian  disinformation maria it's good to have  you with us tonight  you posted a video earlier today from  the center of heave on this ukrainian  independence day and it got our  attention  let's take a look at it  before the invasion putin was saying  that  russian tanks will be on the streets of  kiev in just several days  well here is the main  street in kiev krishaitic and here are  russian tanks  turmobaric weapons all other heavy  russian military equipment burned and  destroyed by ukrainian armed forces and  thousands of people in kiev are now here  making selfies uh in front of the  destroyed russian military vehicles  maria it is clear that you think that  ukraine has won the battle  i'm wondering how can you be sure that  russia will not win this war  the battle is not over unfortunately for  ukraine uh and we have to be prepared  and we are prepared for a long war the  people i have been speaking today with  in khrushchev the the heart of kiev in  maidan square uh all of them say that  ukraine's goal is to get full  territorial control uh of the temporal  occupied territories and that means that  the war is not over for us and it is  continuing but what has changed and what  does this mean this uh uh  so to say uh installation or  exhibition of destroyed russian military  vehicles in kiev what it means for us  ukrainians uh it is the demonstration  that the putin's goal he has put  while he began this invasion on the 24th  of february were not achieved he did not  achieve anything  of the primarily walls and on the other  hand it shows us that ukrainian troops  were able to resist and fight  effectively because many people  including in the west were doubting that  ukrainian army will be able to do so  maria let me speak to you  you as a ukrainian citizen when you look  back at the past six months of this war  how has your life been changed  my personal life has changed completely  before the war i was an analyst and  expert  mainly working from home  and writing research papers desk work as  you would imagine an ordinary expert  will do and from time to time you know  doing trainings and going into  conferences and this war  turned me into some kind of war  correspondent  because now i wear the helmet the body  armor and go to the front lines to show  the world the what is happening in  ukraine and what russia is doing uh to  my country and let me pick up on that i  i know you you made many videos over the  past six months filming the the  aftermath of russian attacks filming  ukrainian troops  has there been a particular setting or  situation that has stayed with you  yes one one thing happened uh just  recently it was in harkee region uh near  my home city uh there is a  small town of chihuahua and  russia stroke the cultural center there  uh with  the s 300 rockets missiles and people  were uh  sheltering in the basement of the  cultural center and three people were  caught inside and we didn't know if  they're alive or not and the rescue  work continued throughout the night and  one of the rescuers  was helped by the uh actually the  husband of the woman which was  there under the rubble and then there  was the scene when the rescuers found  the crushed body and that this uh  he  seen the husband and i talked to him and  he said that they the couple had two  children and he didn't know how to tell  them what has happened to their mother  yeah it's it's  almost impossible to imagine what that  must be like um  are you  worried as the months  turn into a year maybe longer of having  air raid sirens going off at the same  time you have people  um still  taking their children to school going to  cafes  are you worried that  this this dangerous dichotomy of  normalcy and war time life  will get settled in for ukrainians such  as yourself  this is a question i'm asking the people  in the  forefront in the trenches and what they  say me the soldiers they say we are  fighting here for for other people in  ukraine to have their normal lives the  most important thing for ukrainians and  elsewhere in the world is that please do  not forget that this is a war going on  and this is the sovereign state being  attacked by the enemy and while you do  not forget about that and continue  supporting and providing weapons and all  the other help that is uh that ukraine  gets uh ukraine will be able to fight  further and to resist this aggression  maria abdiva we appreciate you taking  the time to share your insights and your  personal stories on this very important  day for ukraine thank you  you

